  "body": "Part of #290. Found while working #532 (Wire EDM: 2-axis contour op).\n\n**What happens:** the Wire Contour op (#532) cuts ONE CLOSED profile, offset to a stated punch / die side. An open profile - a cut-off line, a slot or a relief entered from the stock's edge - is refused by name (\"the profile is open ... an open profile has no punch or die side\"), because punch and die are not defined for a run that encloses nothing.\n\n**Wanted:** an open run cut by the wire, with:\n- the kerf side stated as left / right of travel (or \"on the line\" for a slitting cut, where the kerf is centred) - explicit, never inferred, the same law as punch / die;\n- the wire entering from a stated threading point outside the stock, or from a start hole, and leaving at the run's end (or returning);\n- the same corner minimum on the side the wire rides inside a corner, and the same kerf law (no move brings the wire nearer the kept side than wire radius + gap);\n- both ends of the run stated: where it starts and whether it runs out past the stock.\n\n**Not in this issue:** tabs (#539), taper (#536), skims (#538).\n\n**Open design points** (to settle on this issue before code): how the kept side of an open run is named in the op's parameters next to `side: die | punch`; whether a run that ends inside the material needs a relief hole stated at its end.\n\n**Done means:** an open run cut with the kerf on a stated side stands exactly the kerf offset off the line on that side (pinned by a test and a CL golden); a run with no stated side, and one whose inside corner is tighter than wire radius + gap, are refused by name; the closed-profile goldens of #532 do not move.\n",
